      "role": "user",
      "content": "I’m currently investigating a stability issue in our low-fat mayonnaise prototypes where we are seeing significant flocculation despite maintaining a consistent lecithin concentration. We suspect that the presence of divalent cations, specifically $Ca^{2+}$ from our mineral salts, might be inducing a bridging mechanism that bypasses our electrostatic stabilization. \n\nCan you provide a detailed technical breakdown of how divalent cations interact with the carboxylate and phosphate groups of lecithin to promote bridging flocculation, and how this mechanism specifically overcomes the repulsive forces established by the electrical double layer?",
